{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["The reason for <b>and you shall sacrifice a Passover to HaShem your Elohim, flock and herd</b>, according to the plain sense, is on the order of 'ox, sheep, rams' (Deuteronomy 14:4), 'Reuben, Simeon, Levi' (Exodus 1:2) — that is, an appositional sequence. And he said 'and you shall sacrifice a Passover to HaShem your Elohim — flock and herd'; therefore he did not say 'and you shall sacrifice a Passover to HaShem your Elohim from the flock and from the herd,' as he says everywhere: 'from the herd or from the flock you shall bring your offering' (Leviticus 1:2), and 'if his offering is from the flock' (ibid. v. 10), 'if he offers from the herd' (ibid. 3:1). Rather, he commands here concerning the Passover — and it is the שֶׂה (lamb) that he has already mentioned — while צֹאן וּבָקָר (flock and herd), rams and goats and cattle, are for celebrating a חֲגִיגָה (festive offering) on this day, just as he commands regarding Shavuot and Sukkot: regarding Shavuot he says (Deuteronomy 16:10), 'and you shall make a festival of weeks to HaShem your Elohim according to the freewill offering of your hand' — that one should bring cattle and flock for shelamim (peace offerings) according to what his hand can afford, and likewise regarding Sukkot he says (Deuteronomy 16:15), 'seven days you shall celebrate to HaShem your Elohim,' etc. And at the end he generalizes all three of them together. And similar to this is what we find in Ezekiel (45:21): 'In the first month, on the fourteenth day of the month, you shall have the Passover, a festival of seven days; unleavened bread shall be eaten' — and he explained the festival as seven days. And so we find in the Passover of Josiah, where it says (II Chronicles 35:7): 'sheep, lambs, and kids — all for the Passover offerings — and cattle, three thousand,' and afterward it says (ibid. v. 13): 'and they roasted the Passover with fire according to the ordinance, and the holy things they boiled in pots, in cauldrons, and in pans, and they carried them to all the children of the people.' See, he explained that lambs and kids — all of them — were for the Passover offerings: not goats themselves, and not cattle, as it is written (Exodus 12:5), 'Your lamb shall be without blemish, a male of the first year; you shall take it from the sheep or from the goats.' And he explained that the cattle which he mentioned numbered three thousand, and they were holy things that were in the pots, to distribute before the people to eat to satiety — corresponding to the matter of the חֲגִיגָה (festive offering) that our Rabbis received (Pesahim 70a). And Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Deuteronomy 16:2) rendered: 'prepare flock' — joining the flock with the Passover, and the vav does not connect well with the word וּבָקָר, and furthermore, not all flock is valid for Passover offerings. And the correct interpretation is what we have said.\n\nAnd he explained here many things, for he mentioned concerning the matzah that it shall be 'bread of affliction' (לֶחֶם עֹנִי), to indicate that he commanded to remember that they went out in haste, and it is 'bread of affliction' as a remembrance that they were in Egypt in 'bread of scarcity and water of oppression' — and so it alludes to two things. And thus they said: 'This is the bread of affliction that our fathers ate in the land of Egypt.' Or one may say that it should still be prepared as bread of affliction and not be rich matzah, as our Rabbis mentioned (Pesahim 36a). And he explained the commandment concerning se'or (leaven) — that it shall not be seen throughout all your territory — because at first he said (Exodus 12:19), 'it shall not be found in your houses.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The verse citation in the phrase 'וּבַסּוֹף כָּלַל אֶת שְׁלָשְׁתָּם' refers to Deuteronomy 16:16-17, where all three pilgrimage festivals are gathered together at the end of the chapter."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:43.816904377Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}